    "result_text": "Terms now count business days. A claim's due-by is the terms-th business day after its date, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and the sheet's holidays. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`HolidayService`:** the old rule (date + terms, then pushed past any holidays) is replaced by `dueBy(sheetId, date, terms)`, which does the business-day count. New claims and split-off parts both use it. If terms are 0, the due-by is the claim's own date; I made that choice because \"0th business day after\" isn't defined.\n- **Everything that reads the due-by** (overdue, the late charge's boundaries, the period a claim belongs to) already read the stored due-by, so none of it needed changing. A holiday added later still leaves existing due-bys alone, because the due-by is fixed when the claim is added.\n- **Claims already on the sheet:** a new migration, `V53__business_day_due_by.java`, recalculates every claim's due-by from its date and the sheet's holidays at the time it runs. It's a Java Flyway migration registered as a Spring bean; the startup log shows all 53 migrations validated, so it is picked up.\n\n**One thing to know about the migration:** claims don't store the terms they were fixed with, so the migration works them out from the old due-by. It takes the number of days from the claim's date to its due-by, minus any holidays the due-by was pushed past. When a holiday falls just before the old due-by, it can't tell \"terms ran onto the holiday and got pushed\" from \"terms happened to land on the next day\", and it assumes the first. The tests don't cover this, because the app's in-memory H2 database starts empty on every run. If you want this exact for real data, the fix would be to store each claim's terms in its own column.\n\nI also updated the comments that described the old rule (on `Claim.dueBy`, `HolidayService`, and in the front-end claims and holidays files). I left the old comment in `V52__holiday.sql` as it was, because editing an applied migration changes its checksum and Flyway would then refuse to start.",
